A||||19||terryk||4T60E in a 84-87 Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||terryk||08-13-2000||05:18 PM||||Ok, so if anyone has any information on this, I'd love to heard it. I started working out the details.<P>So far, I've found that to installa 4T60E or 65E in a Fiero requires two changes.<P>1. The 4T60E inner tripot housings must be swapped in place of the Fiero housings.<P>2. The passenger side needs a shorter axle shaft and it *appears* that the driver's side is the correct length.<P>Can anyone confirm this?<P>Regards<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||08-14-2000||03:18 PM||||Looks like I'm on my own on this. I searched the archives and it appears that no one has actually done this swap.<P>Well, I'll have the details in a few days.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-14-2000||03:22 PM||||Someone around here did know. check in archives it's been some time and maybe they haven't been here in awhile.<P>As I remember they said the only thing you needed was a custom lenght right axle. The left one wasn't an issue. The tranny mounting is all the same as TH125 other wise. I think even the TC lockup connector is the same.<P>If you can't get he axle local try CV's unlimited. They are online <A HREF="http://www.cvunlimited.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cvunlimited.com/</A>  and I'm told they custom work stuff. Haven't dealt with them myself.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||08-14-2000||04:16 PM||||Terry,<P>This is the kind of info I've been looking for. In the couple of links I've posted, I was only able to get vague info on what "should" or "might" work. Please keep us posted on your progress and all the details. This would make a great addition to the "On-Line Service Guide" ( <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/osg.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/osg.html</A>  ) in the "Modifications"  section.<P>Thanks in advance for your hard work,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000004||rockcrawl||08-14-2000||06:40 PM||||Terry,<BR> I'm using the 4T60 with my 4.9 conversion, but I'm also using the Cad cradle/axles/brakes etc., so I can't give you much info. The 4T60 is the trans that is used with the 3800, so anyone who's done a 3800/4sp auto conversion should be able to clue you in on axles and mounts. I've heard that the axles from a Pontiac Transport minivan are what you need, they are direct fit. The Fiero shifter can be easily modified to work with the 4 speed auto by filing or grinding another detent notch on it, I've done that for a 325-4L conversion. Are you useing the 4T60E with the 2.8? The 4T60E is fully electronic and requires a computer to operate, unlike the earlier (pre '91)4T60.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||08-15-2000||12:08 AM||||Theogre,<P>Actually, CV Unlimited is where I got the axles for my 1986 Ciera GT Supercharge swap. Great prices.<P>This swap is a 1992 Buick 3800 SC with the 4T60E into an 86 Fiero. I have the engine and trans sitting on the cradle now using 1987 Ciera 4spd auto transaxle brackets. The crank/engine mount doesn't line up with the cradle mount (I didn't expect it too). Now to see which N or A car had the offset engine mount. <P>Once that engine mount is resolved, I can start to measure the axle lengths better.<P>I just sat the 4T60E and 92 3800SC on the cradle. I used the Fiero mounts and A car trans brackets. The trans side cover (stamped steel, not the cast type), is just about touching the cradle on the driver's side. I think it will clear if I move the whole engine/trans to the passenger side. The slots in the cradles should be enough. On the rear mount, I used the holes closet to the drivers side. There are two pairs of holes there.<P>The crank side has the expected problem using the A car crank mount. It is to far to the front. Unless an N or other A car has an offset mount, it looks like I will have to extend the cradle plate where the mount will sit towards the front.<P>87GTZ34, did you use the outside most holes for the rear trans mount on the cradle?<P>Terry<P><BR>Regards<P>Terry<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-15-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-29-2000||01:32 PM||||Terryk,<P>Got any freash updates to your automatic OD conversion project?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000007||terryk||08-29-2000||03:11 PM||||I would if the engine bracket would arrive. <P>I've spent so much time measuring things, I should just modify the A car bracket and continue on. If it doesn't arrive today, I'll just cut the A car engine mount and mod it to clear the stock SC oil filter adapter.<P>At this point it appears the cradle slots for the trans mounts (A car) will need to be lengthened to the passenger side maybe an inch or so. It's hard to position the engine properly with the engine mount.<P>The U-van axle combination does appear to be correct. CVUnlimited said $89 per side (U-van axle with manual trans Fiero outer CV/spline). Easy solution.<P>Otherwise, yep, it will fit. Once I have it all together, I'll detail it out more.<P>Terry<P>I edited out the reference to the company name.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-01-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000008||rockcrawl||08-29-2000||05:17 PM||||Terry,<BR> Please keep us informed as progress is made. I'm going to be installing a 2.8/4T60 combo in my 87 GT. Once you have everything complete, let us have all the details!  <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||10
Z||000009||terryk||08-29-2000||05:34 PM||||Will do. Keep in mind that the 4T60 is a bit shorter than the 4T60E on the passenger side. You'll need a longer axle although the 125C axles might work.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FIEROCIOUS||08-30-2000||02:29 AM||||Would the 4T80E and 4T60 be comaparable in length? ||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||08-30-2000||11:51 AM||||I've never measured a 4T80E. I seen many. My guess is they are longer side to side, but I can't confirm that.  <P>Go test drive an STS and let us know!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Sillhouette.com||08-30-2000||08:57 PM||||just wondering why use a 4T60E transmission.  They are torque limited to 210HP so no more engine mods especially if you're using a 3.4DOHC engine.  Just an idea use a five speed manual tranny with your engine and that would also get rid of your programming problems for a turbo setup.  <BR>The 4T80 tranny is larger than the 4T60E I wish they were the same size I would have had it in my GTP 3.4DOHC a long time ago.<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||08-30-2000||11:46 PM||||For me it's because the 4T60E is a great trans, the SC engines run better on it, in California you can't put an SC on a manual and get certified (save the California Smog Nazi jokes) and I can change the 92-95 calibrations. <P>I prefer an auto on a blown engine. The DOHC needs the manual to feel like it has some low-end. A DOHC on a 4T60E is a bit slow on the low end. The 92-95 SC with a 4T60E is a sweet combination.<P>Yes, the 4T60E is limited on 3-4 upshift but that's not much of an issue. The 4T65E is rated the same for torque as the 4T60E: 280 ft-lbs. And what is the Getrag *rated*?<P>4T60E is a nice trans. Don't be 'disin' the 4T60E! Joking, ok?<P>At the same time, I'd love to drive an SC on a Getrag just to see what it's like. Sounds like the results are mixed.<P>Terry<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-30-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000014||Tim||08-31-2000||10:30 PM||||terryk<BR>A few years ago I researched axles to swap the 125 with a 440.  I used an interchange book to get the "number" for Fiero 125 axles, then crossed that number to the list of cars that used the axles.  Check which cars came with the auto-4 and standard brakes(not heavy duty) and you have found stock axles.  BTW: Pontiac 6000 3sp auto with standard brakes uses the same axles as the Fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||08-31-2000||11:28 PM||||Hi Tim, that's good information. I suspected the Fiero width matched one of the other cars. If it matches the 6000, then it matches the Ciera and I have an extra set of those HD axles.<P>Life's looking good!<P>And no, the Fiero does not use the Citation suspension. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Couldn't resist.||||reg||1
Z||000016||FIEROCIOUS||09-01-2000||01:38 AM||||What transverse auto tranny has the highest torque capacity?<BR>What is available for the 2.8 bolt pattern?<BR>What is available for the SBC bolt pattern?<BR>What is available for the N*s bolt pattern? ||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||09-01-2000||02:05 AM||||From Generous Motors?<P>My understanding and according to the Powertrain website is:<P>4T80E - 305 ft-lbs<BR>4T60E/4T65E - 280ft-lbs<BR>THM440-T4 - 235 ft-lbs<BR>4T45E - 220 ft-lbs<BR>3T40/4T40E - 200ft-lbs (125C)<P>As far as bellhousing patterns, beats me. I know there is no transaxle with a SBC pattern. The 4T80E might be the same as the 4T6n's. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||09-01-2000||11:36 PM||||The bracket arrived.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000019||terryk||09-09-2000||04:38 PM||||Through the miracle of modern pouting, I got the engine bracket from West Coast Fieros. Thanks Chris! Nice bracket.<P>I installed the bracket on the 3800SC and watched where the trans mounts wanted to rest. As Chris indicated (seeing is believing for me), the front mount dropped into the inner most cradle slots on. <P>The rear mount needed new holes in the cradle. Turns out, I just had to cut a new set of slots just like the front side. I should have remember this from the Ciera installation.<P>I set the engine back in and everything lined up. <P>Now I can verify the axles, but early measurements confirm again Command West's statements: Usethe U/Dustbuster van (94 and up 4T60E) axles with the Fiero outer CV/spindle dropped on. Several places said they would build this combination if I wanted it.<P>So to mount the 4T60E in a Fiero cradle I used:<P>1. All three stock Fiero rubber mounts.<BR>2. A body trans brackets from a 4T60/440-T4.<BR>3. WCF's 3800 engine mount.<BR>4. Created a set of matching slots in the back cradle mount location (match the distance on the front mount).<P>Axle verification next....<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||14
